    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$2.11 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e for a full roast dinner with gravy. Vegetables and potatoes are cheap, the loin goes on sale constantly, and the gravy is made from what was already stuck to the pot.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eWatch for whole pork loin on sale and buy the biggest one.\u003c/strong\u003e Whole loin is one of the most reliably discounted cuts in the meat case, and it runs far under the price of precut roasts.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eApple juice instead of wine.\u003c/strong\u003e It deglazes the pot just as well, adds a touch of sweetness to the gravy, and costs a small fraction of even a cheap bottle.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy carrots, celery and potatoes in bags and bunches.\u003c/strong\u003e This pot takes a lot of all three, and the bag price per pound is where the savings live.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eThe gravy costs about a nickel.\u003c/strong\u003e Flour, butter and the drippings you already have. There is no reason to buy a jar or a packet for this."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Pat the pork loin dry, season it hard with salt, black pepper, dried thyme and Italian seasoning, and sear it on every side in olive oil in a heavy Dutch oven until deeply browned.",
                    "Lift the roast out. Cook the chopped onion, carrots and celery in the same pot until they start to color, then stir in the minced garlic and the bay leaves.",
                    "Pour in the apple juice, scrape every browned bit off the bottom, then add the chicken broth.",
                    "Set the roast back on the vegetables, tuck the quartered potatoes around it, cover, and roast at 325F for about 90 minutes until the pork is tender.",
                    "Move the roast and the vegetables to a board and let the meat rest 15 minutes before you slice or shred it.",
                    "Set the pot over medium heat, whisk the flour into the butter to make a paste, stir it into the drippings, and simmer until the gravy thickens. Season and pull the bay leaves."
                ],
